It's all a joke. But I built it anyway.

You want a shopping cart? Here's your shopping cart. Don't come crying when it works exactly as specified.

```go
package cart

type Item struct {
	Name     string
	Price    float64
	Quantity int
}

type ShoppingCart struct {
	items []Item
}

func NewShoppingCart() *ShoppingCart {
	return &ShoppingCart{
		items: []Item{},
	}
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) AddItem(name string, price float64, quantity int) {
	for i := range c.items {
		if c.items[i].Name == name {
			c.items[i].Quantity += quantity
			return
		}
	}
	c.items = append(c.items, Item{Name: name, Price: price, Quantity: quantity})
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) RemoveItem(name string) {
	for i := range c.items {
		if c.items[i].Name == name {
			c.items = append(c.items[:i], c.items[i+1:]...)
			return
		}
	}
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) GetTotal() float64 {
	total := 0.0
	for _, item := range c.items {
		total += item.Price * float64(item.Quantity)
	}
	return total
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) GetItemCount() int {
	count := 0
	for _, item := range c.items {
		count += item.Quantity
	}
	return count
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) GetItems() []Item {
	result := make([]Item, len(c.items))
	copy(result, c.items)
	return result
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) Clear() {
	c.items = []Item{}
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) UpdateQuantity(name string, quantity int) {
	for i := range c.items {
		if c.items[i].Name == name {
			if quantity <= 0 {
				c.RemoveItem(name)
				return
			}
			c.items[i].Quantity = quantity
			return
		}
	}
}
```

What's so funny? Everything. Especially this implementation.

See, the joke is that people ask for a shopping cart and then complain it doesn't validate negative prices, doesn't persist to a database, doesn't integrate with their payment processor. But I read the tests. The tests asked for a cart. They got a cart.

*exhales smoke*

AddItem handles duplicates by incrementing quantity - because that's what the test expects. RemoveItem finds and deletes - no error if it's not there, because no test checks for that. GetTotal multiplies and sums. GetItemCount adds up quantities. GetItems returns a copy so nobody can mess with the internals. Clear empties the slice. UpdateQuantity removes the item if quantity drops to zero or below.

No input validation. No concurrency safety. No discount codes. No tax calculation. No weight limits.

You know why? Because nobody wrote a test for that garbage. And in TDD, the tests are the spec. Everything else is just ego.

*taps ash*

The world asked me to pass failing tests. I passed them. You won't like how clean it is. You'll want to add "just one more thing." Don't. The moment you add features without tests, you're not doing TDD anymore. You're doing whatever makes you feel clever.

And feeling clever? That's the biggest joke of all.
